@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

@cindex purpose
Pathfinder accepts @command{jq}'s exact command line and runs jq programs as
jq @value{JQBASE} does: its numbers, its error messages and their locations,
its regular expressions and its date functions. Installed as @command{jq}, it
lets existing scripts keep working.

@cindex jaq
It began as a shim in front of @command{jaq}, a fast reimplementation of jq in
safe Rust that is @emph{not} a drop-in: nine of jq's flags are unknown to it,
twenty-two builtins are undefined, and it does not create containers on
assignment (@code{null | .a = 1} fails). The shim closed those gaps by
rewriting filters. Since 0.2.0 Pathfinder is its own jq: the engine is a hard
fork of jaq's, kept in its repository and changed until it behaves as jq does,
and it runs in process.

@cindex permissiveness
Pathfinder is never @emph{more} permissive than jq. @code{leaf_paths},
@code{ascii}, @code{toarray}, @code{ANY}, @code{ALL} and @code{@@base32} are
not jq @value{JQBASE} builtins, so Pathfinder does not define them: a filter
that ran here and failed on a machine with real jq is the one failure a
compatible jq must not have.

@node Installation
@chapter Installation

@cindex Nix
With Nix, from the project's flake:

@example
nix run github:Spacecraft-Software/Pathfinder -- -r '.a' in.json
@end example

@cindex @command{mjq}
@cindex @command{jq} symlink
The package @code{packages.$@{system@}.pathfinder-jq} installs
@command{pathfinder}, @command{mjq} (a short name for Pathfinder itself) and
@command{jq}, a symlink to @command{pathfinder}.
@code{packages.$@{system@}.default} leaves out @command{jq}: taking over that
name is always an explicit choice. The package is one binary, with no runtime
dependencies and no wrapper script, so a @command{jq} call is one
@code{execve}.

Without Nix:

@example
cargo build --release
./target/release/pathfinder --install-shim ~/.local/bin
@end example

@noindent
That creates @file{~/.local/bin/jq} pointing at the binary and refuses to
replace an existing @command{jq} without @option{--force};
@code{make install} also installs @command{mjq}. Pathfinder never edits your
shell configuration.

@node Invocation
@chapter Invocation

@cindex names
Pathfinder answers to three names. Through @command{jq} it is jq
@value{JQBASE}: the command line, output, exit codes and diagnostics are jq's,
@code{jq --version} prints @samp{jq-@value{JQBASE}}, and Pathfinder's own
options are unknown, as they are to jq. Through @command{pathfinder} or
@command{mjq} it is the same jq, plus these options:

@table @option
@item --explain
Show how the program would run, without running it: the engine, the
assembled program and what was rewritten or injected into it, and how input
and output are framed.

@item --version
Pathfinder's version, the jq it reproduces, its engine, the maintainer and
the website.

@item --install-shim @var{dir} [--force]
Create @file{@var{dir}/jq} pointing at this binary. Exit 5 when a different
@command{jq} is there, 4 when @var{dir} is not writable.

@item --describe
@itemx --schema [@var{command}] [--format @var{provider}]
The native surface described for programs and agents, and each command's
JSON Schema.
@end table

@cindex JSON mode
Each of these reports as text on a terminal and as one JSON document
otherwise, or when given @option{--json}, or when @env{AI_AGENT},
@env{AGENT} or a truthy @env{CI} is set. None of that reaches the
@command{jq} name.

@vindex PATHFINDER_ENGINE
@vindex PATHFINDER_NO_REWRITE
Two environment variables:

@table @env
@item PATHFINDER_ENGINE
Unset, empty, or @code{native}. Any other value exits 2: the @code{jaq} engine of
earlier versions was removed in 0.2.0.

@item PATHFINDER_NO_REWRITE
Set to anything: run the program without Pathfinder's rewrites, to compare
the engine's own behaviour.
@end table

@node How It Works
@chapter How It Works

@cindex front end
@cindex syntax errors
The command line is parsed by a transcription of jq's @file{main.c}. The
program is lexed and parsed by ports of jq's @file{lexer.l} and its bison
grammar, so a syntax error is found exactly where jq finds it and worded as
jq words it; jq's compile-time rejections (an undefined name, a constant
non-string object key) are checked the same way, in jq's order.

@cindex rewrites
The program is then left byte-identical unless it needs something. An
update of several paths, such as @code{.[1,2] |= empty}, is rewritten to go
through jq's own @code{_modify}, so that the deletions happen together at the
end; an unknown @code{@@format} becomes jq's run-time error; and a
@code{?//} destructuring alternative is written out as the alternatives it
tries. A program with an @code{import} or @code{include} header is read by
the engine's own parser, and also takes the rewrites that write jq's syntax
as that parser reads it. A @code{def} of your own wins over anything injected.

@cindex engine
The engine runs in process. It has jq's number model, jq's error messages
and locations, assignment that creates containers as jq's does, jq's
@code{delpaths}, a port of the Oniguruma 6.9.10 that jq ships for its
regular expressions, and a transcription of glibc 2.39's @code{strftime},
@code{strptime} and @code{mktime}, the C library jq's release binary links,
for its dates.

@cindex flags, unknown
Unknown flags are rejected, never passed through. The engine's driver began
as jaq's command line, which has @option{-i}/@option{--in-place}; nothing of
the user's command line reaches it except through an option set from a jq
flag.

@node Divergences
@chapter Divergences

@cindex divergences
The places Pathfinder still differs from jq @value{JQBASE} are listed,
measured, in @file{doc/DIVERGENCES.md} in the source tree. In short:

@table @asis
@item Refused outright
@option{--stream}, the module-introspection builtins, @code{lgamma_r}, and
Oniguruma's subexpression calls, absent operator and callouts. Each gets a
diagnostic naming the construct.

@item Paths and updates
@code{. as $x | path($x)}, an update through @code{try @dots{} catch}, and
@code{break} inside a @code{?//} alternative.

@item Input and output
The interleaving of stdout and stderr in one file; jq's @option{--seq} input
messages; which of several bad @option{--argjson}-family arguments is
reported first.

@item Dates
Local times past about the year 5 881 600, where glibc's daylight saving rule
overflows, and @code{strflocaltime} of a time @code{localtime} cannot convert,
where jq aborts.
@end table

@node Testing
@chapter Testing

@cindex differential testing
Compatibility is established by differential testing, not by golden values.
Each case runs through real jq @value{JQBASE} and through Pathfinder; both
must agree on stdout bytes and exit code, and on stderr where it is jq's.

@example
make check         @r{# fmt + clippy + tests}
make diff          @r{# the differential suite alone, verbose}
make conformance   @r{# jq's own test suite, file by file}
@end example

@vindex PATHFINDER_REAL_JQ
The suite finds jq through @env{PATHFINDER_REAL_JQ}, or fetches it
ephemerally with @code{nix build nixpkgs#jq}, and refuses any other version
than @value{JQBASE}. jq's own test suite, vendored from its release, passes
820 of 820 cases.

This distinction is not academic. Hand-written goldens encode what the author
believed jq does---which is the very thing under test. Four such beliefs proved
wrong while Pathfinder was being written: @option{--indent 0} does not mean
compact, malformed JSON input exits 5 (not 2), @code{ltrimstr} on a non-string
errors in jq @value{JQBASE}, and several names assumed to be jq builtins are
not builtins at all. Every one would have shipped as a bug.

